If it��s true that we are what we eat then migraine sufferers have to be very careful what they put in their mouth since certain foods could trigger an excruciating headache that could leave them holed up in a dark, quiet room until the pain subsides.
Based on surveys conducted in 2010 and 2011, Statistics Canada reported an estimated 2.7 million Canadians suffer from migraines, a debilitating disorder characterized by throbbing headaches that can last anywhere from a few hours to several days. Nausea, vomiting and sensitivity to light and sound are often part of the package.
More than half of the ��migraineurs�� said their severe headaches had prevented them from driving while 76 per cent weren��t able to get a good night��s sleep. Depression, social isolation and sick days off work were listed as other common symptoms.
Since fluctuations in estrogen can trigger migraines, women are more likely to suffer from them than men, often before or after their periods when their estrogen levels take a nosedive. Other possible causes include stress, anxiety, lack of sleep and even changes to the weather. For folks living in Calgary, those warm Chinook winds on frosty days can feel mind-blowing — literally.
But the numbers of migraine sufferers could be significantly higher than the national figures indicated since many go undiagnosed. According to the Ontario Migraine Clinic in Georgetown, Ont., ��this disease is grossly misunderstood and misdiagnosed.�� The centre, which uses a unique method of acupuncture to treat migraine patients, predicts as many as ��one in four households in Canada have someone affected by migraine headaches.��
Everything from perfume and cigarette smoke to humidity, dehydration and sex can trigger migraines, but the root of the problem is baffling. According to the Mayo Clinic, ��although much about the cause of migraines isn��t understood, genetics and environmental factors appear to play a role. Migraines may be caused by changes in the brainstem and its interactions with the trigeminal nerve, a major pain pathway. Imbalances in brain chemicals, including serotonin, which helps regulate pain in your nervous system, also may be involved.��
Research also shows links between migraines and food. ��Migraine is a complex neurobiochemical process, meaning it affects the nervous and biochemical systems of the body,�� says Headache Network Canada, an organization that provides information and education on the treatment of headache disorders. ��Food is the fuel the makes our engines run. A person prone to migraine attacks needs to pay extra attention to the daily maintenance of their engines (their bodies) through their dietary habits and nutritional intake.��
Some of the worst offenders are caffeine, booze and processed meats, but even healthy foods such as oranges, yogurt, bananas and string beans, can ignite a tsunami in your head.
��Foods that we eat cause a cascade of physiological events to use the nutrients we digest. The different nutrients cause different neurotransmitters to be released so that the body can perform the correct function to utilize the food properly. This balancing of transmitters is different with different foods. Foods that are referred to as a trigger, challenge the individual��s ability to balance transmitters in the correct amount. The inappropriate transmitter balance leads to the symptoms of migraine, �� the Ontario Migraine Clinic explains.
While there is no known cure for migraines, there are preventive measures that can be taken to minimize the number and intensity of the attacks. Health experts recommend keeping a diary to determine patterns and possible triggers and changing your diet to avoid common food offenders.
��Some people can avoid migraines by knowing their triggers, like aged cheese, chocolate or alcohol. If you suffer from migraines, take note of what you eat and drink before the start of your migraine. It may help you avoid migraines in the future,�� says Eat Right Ontario.
Common migraine triggers
MSG-laced foods: Monosodium glutamate is a flavour enhancer often used in Asian dishes, canned soups, pickles, salad dressings and cured meats.
Processed meats: Hot dogs, bacon and deli meats all contain nitrites, a common trigger for migraines.
Caffeine: Chocolate, tea, coffee, soft drinks and energy drinks
Red wine: A favourite for many, but it contains tannins, which can cause migraines.
Dried fruit: Figs, prunes and apricots, which contain sulfites, another common trigger.
Bananas: Though a good source of potassium, bananas are high in tyramine, an amino acid that reduces serotonin levels in the brain causing headaches.
