How did it go so wrong?
That��s the question many Padres fans are asking after the team posted a 74-88 record, its worst showing in four seasons.
Many thought the retooled Padres would be a playoff team in 2015. Even those who didn��t, figured they��d at least have a winning record.
ESPN projected before the season the Padres would go 88-74. Baseball Prospectus had them at 83-79. So did Grantland and Sports Illustrated. The Westgate Las Vegas Superbook set the Padres over/under win total at 84.5.
Instead it was another losing season, and the worst one since 2011 at that. Here is how and why 2015 was a disaster for the Padres, and by implication what needs to be fixed.
WASTED AT-BATS
The Padres finished last in the majors in batting average (.243) and on-base percentage (.300). Even adjusting for park effects, the website Fangraphs.com ranked them the second-worst offense in baseball.
It wasn��t the headline players who failed. Matt Kemp and Justin Upton largely did their jobs with 20-plus homers, 80-plus RBI and OPS��s above.750. Yangervis Solarte posted a.270 batting average with 33 doubles, 14 home runs and 63 RBI. Yonder Alonso (.361 OBP) and Wil Myers (.763 OPS) were both effective at the plate when healthy. Melvin Upton Jr. (.757 OPS) had his best season in years.
Despite all that, the Padres offense remained abysmal because they wasted too many at-bats on players who simply can��t hit. Alexi Amarista, Clint Barmes, Will Middlebrooks and Jedd Gyorko received a combined 1,309 plate appearances. None entered the season with career averages above.250, OBPs above.300 or OPS��s above.700. Predictably, all finished under those modest marks this year. Add the 221 plate appearances for underprepared rookies Austin Hedges and Travis Jankowski – both of whom had fewer than 100 Triple-A at-bats – and the Padres essentially gave away three to four free outs every time through the lineup any given day. No matter what defensive qualities those players bring or what the top of the order does, that��s just too big a shortcoming to overcome.
LACK OF LEFTIES
The Padres started a right-handed pitcher in 159 of their 162 games and went long stretches with only right-handers in the bullpen, and the result was left-handed hitters pounded the Padres staff. Lefties posted a.256/.338/.442 slash line and a major league-high 98 home runs against the Padres. Compare that to the tame 248/.305/.388 slash line right-handed hitters managed, and it��s clear the Padres�� lack of left-handed pitching options hurt them badly. Opponents simply stacked their lineups with lefties – the Padres faced left-handed hitters 2,970 times this season, the most in baseball, and nearly 300 more times than league average – and let them rake, and for most of the season there was nothing the Padres could do to counter it.
DEFENSIVE DECISIONS
Defense was never going to be this team��s strength, but the Padres made it worse by unnecessarily moving capable defenders to unfamiliar positions.
Catcher Derek Norris, second baseman Cory Spangenberg and third baseman Solarte are all rated above average at their positions, yet spent at least 108 innings at other spots, where they rated poorly. Myers proved capable in left and right field and scouts predict he has a future at first base, but he played more innings in center field than anywhere, even when the Padres had openings at other spots and other players who could play center.
By scrambling the entire defense every time one player got hurt – rather than just bring up a player who actually played the position – the Padres took a bad defense and made it worse. For example, they used 10 players at first base, when in reality all they needed was Alonso, Myers and, when they got hurt, Brett Wallace to hold it down. Various advanced analytics ranked the Padres consistently one of the five worst defenses in baseball, and they brought most of it on themselves.
MANAGERIAL TURMOIL
Despite all the aforementioned flaws, the Padres weren��t as bad as their record indicates. They went 32-33 their first 65 games playing 10 more games against above-.500 teams than anyone else. They were 33-32 from July 11 until the final 10-day stretch of the season.
That��s a.500 team over 130 games. The problem was a three-week stretch in early summer when they went 7-16, which coincides with the moment they fired Bud Black. The Padres were competitive against a tough schedule through their first 65 games with Black and were about to embark on the easiest part of their schedule the morning he was let go: 11 games against struggling Oakland, Arizona and Seattle and middling San Francisco. Right when the Padres had the opportunity to rack up wins, management pulled the trigger and set the clubhouse – which widely respected Black – back. It took a while for players to jell with interim manager Pat Murphy, and by the time they did, the 7-16 stretch was over.
