PHILADELPHIA — The Phillies walked-off with a 2-1 win in 15 innings early Wednesday morning and there's no way that would have been possible without their bullpen.
After more struggles recently, the bullpen rebounded in a big way, throwing eight shutout innings, allowing one hit and striking out 14.
Ken Giles, Jonathan Papelbon and Jake Diekman needed 80 pitches to get through three innings but they got the job done. Justin De Fratus, Mario Hollands and Hector Neris — he earned his first career major league win — each shut down the Astros for an inning as well.
But Phillies manager Ryne Sandberg gave the most praise to Antonio Bastardo, who pitched two innings and struck out six of the seven batters he faced.
"Last night, that was as good as I've seen him throw all year, with a real good fastball at 94 [mph], and then his other stuff was even better because of his fastball," Sandberg said. "But, yeah, he really made a nice turnaround from like 10 days ago. The stuff is just overall crisper and a lot better."
Bastardo, who was tied to a number of trade rumors last month, had two rough outings before the July 31 trade deadline but has rebounded with three straight scoreless outings.
Revere not a worry
Ben Revere sacrifices his body several times a week crashing into outfield walls or diving to make a catch. Typically he gets up grimacing or limping and last night was another one of those nights.
Sandberg, however, wasn't concerned Wednesday and said he's been told that Revere can't do any more damage to his right ankle, which he broke last July.
"He's been reassured that he cannot [make things worse]," Sandberg said. "I check with him every day after I watch his first at-bat and he limps around and I think, 'Holy Cow, first at-bat?' But you know what, he seems to bounce back each day with a new day. But he's been reassured he can't do anything worse, it's just a tolerance.
"He seems to tolerate it fine. It seems to be when he goes full speed he tolerates it fine, but when he slows up, the slower pace, then he feels it. But all in all it doesn't affect the way he's swinging the bat or running the bases."
In fact, he's actually been producing more at the plate lately. Heading into Wednesday's game he was hitting.362 over his last 13 games, scoring seven runs. He's also over.300 on the season, batting.303.
