Media The Observer Who gave £350,000 to save Edward's failing TV firm? What the chairman said Royal row as Ardent chairman admits he does not know source of cash from tax haven
Prince Edward and his wife, the Countess of Wessex, were facing fresh questions over their business links yesterday after The Observer discovered that the prince's TV company had 'no idea' who had provided a £350,000 lump sum to save it.
In a remarkable interview, Malcolm Cockren, chairman of Ardent Productions, agreed that the money, from a firm based in an offshore tax haven, could have been provided by the Mafia or disgraced dictators. Asked directly if he knew where the money came from, he replied: 'No.'
Questioned further, he said: 'If the bank was about to foreclose on your mortgage and somebody offered you £350,000, would you turn it down?' Buckingham Palace also said it did not know where the money came from.
The Observer can also reveal that the countess still plays a role in the public relations firm that caused controversy when it was the subject of a'sting' by the News of the World. In 2001 she was entrapped into offering 'royal access for cash' to a potential client proposing a £20,000-a-month contract. He turned out to be a reporter dressed as a sheikh. She said on tape that there was an 'unspoken benefit' for her clients.
The incident led to a review by Lord Luce, the Lord Chamberlain, into commercial activity by members of the extended royal family. In April 2001 the countess said she would'step aside as chairman of RJH'. The latest company documents disclose that she still is a non-executive director and owns a third of the firm. A spokeswoman for RJH said: 'The Countess of Wessex rarely comes into the office. She is not involved, does not meet clients and does not receive any remuneration.'
The firm's clients include Teletext, JCB and the Lanesborough hotel in central London. This year, the countess was photographed in Hello! magazine celebrating her 40th birthday at the Lanesborough.
Three years ago both the prince and his wife announced they were standing down from their private companies after a string of controversies that saw them accused of cashing in on their status. The Queen agreed to pay them £250,000 a year to compensate the couple for their lost salaries.
Yet documents recently filed at Companies House reveal that both still retain active financial interests in the companies, though neither receives any wages. The documents show that the prince's firm received the injection of funds from a company based in the British Virgin Islands, the offshore tax haven that has a reputation for secrecy. Cockren said the money originally came in the form of a loan over five years to help finance some of Ardent's films but was transferred into shares in 2002.
The prince remains a director of Ardent Productions, the independent TV company he set up in 1993, which made a number of films about the royal family. Although it racked up losses of £2 million and ceased productions, its subsidiary, Ardent International Sales, receives royalties from the films the prince's company made.
The only information provided by Ardent about the company behind the cash injection is that it is called Intercap Ventures Incorporated and is located at PO Box 3174, Road Town, Tortola.
Ian Davidson, a Labour member of the Commons public accounts committee, said Ardent's lack of knowledge was not good enough.
'The royal family has a duty to be as open as they can about their finances,' he said. 'If the son of the Queen is in business with a British Virgin Islands company, we need to know who is behind this firm. Was this individual or individuals trying to buy access to the royal family?'
Davidson's view is backed by Richard Murphy, a financial expert from the Tax Justice Network, which is campaigning against the negative impact of offshore tax havens.
'There are two reasons an individual uses the British Virgin Islands: to avoid paying tax or to hide their identity,' he said. 'I don't believe it is appropriate that a member of the royal family, funded by the civil list, receives funding from a source disguised by the use of an offshore company where secrecy always prevails.
'What does it say about his willingness to be open about the source of its funding and the trade it is derived from? Why does he wish to associate with people who do not wish to acknowledge their association with him? What does this say about his attitude towards corporate responsibility and the agenda of accountability inherent in that?'
Edward set up Ardent boasting it would be one of the top 12 independent product firms by 2000 and vowed he would not trade on his status. Yet almost from the moment it began it was embroiled in controversy as it made money only from films about the royal family. Its one success was a documentary about the restoration of Windsor Castle. It also caused much embarrassment when it filmed Prince William as he started at St Andrews University.
